.Dd November 13, 2026
.Dt DEBUGFS 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm debugfs
.Nd the kernel's debug files under /sys/kernel/debug
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The kernel mounts debugfs at
.Pa /sys/kernel/debug
at boot, with
.Ql nosuid ,
.Ql nodev
and
.Ql noexec ,
where Linux systems mount it, and the mount cannot be taken away.
Its files are those the kernel's subsystems register, as Linux's are,
for test programs such as IGT's to drive.
.Pp
The root directory is root's, with mode 0700, as Linux makes it, so no
other user looks below it:
an open, a
.Xr stat 2
or a listing of anything below it by any other user fails with
.Er EACCES .
A directory is root's with mode 0755, a file root's with the mode its
subsystem gives it, and a symbolic link root's with mode 0777.
A file's size is 0, whatever it reads, until a truncation, which
.Xr open 2
with
.Dv O_TRUNC
makes, sets the size that
.Xr stat 2
reports and nothing else, as on Linux.
The times of a node are those of its making.
.Pa /sys/kernel/debug
is a mount of its own, with a device number of its own, and its
.Pa ..
is
.Pa /sys/kernel .
.Xr statfs 2
reports
.Dv DEBUGFS_MAGIC ,
0x64626720.
Nothing else makes, removes, renames or changes a node there;
those calls fail with
.Er EROFS
or
.Er EPERM .
.Pp
Most files are text, as Linux's seq files are: a read from offset 0 makes
the text anew, and later reads give the rest of it from the description's
offset, which
.Xr lseek 2
moves by
.Dv SEEK_SET
and
.Dv SEEK_CUR ;
.Dv SEEK_END
fails with
.Er EINVAL .
A write to a text file that takes none fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
.Xr mount 2
places another debugfs mount, which shows the same files; its options
.Ql mode= ,
in octal,
.Ql uid=
and
.Ql gid=
set the root directory's mode and owner for every mount, as on Linux.
.Ss Files
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa files/creators
Mode 0400, a Rach2 file with no Linux counterpart.
Each line is the decimal process id of the thread group that allocated
one live open file description, in the initial PID namespace;
0 means that no process was current at allocation.
There is no header.
The kernel walks the description list in batches of 32, holding its lock
for one batch at a time, so the text is not one atomic snapshot:
a description live through the whole read is listed once,
one allocated during it is not, and one released during it may be.
The id stays with the description through dup, fork and transfers,
and after its creator exits, until its last reference goes.
It is an allocation's creator, not its current holder;
process ids may be reused.
The description used to read this file is included.
This lets a test count its own allocations after their descriptors
and objects have closed, without counting other processes' files.
.It Pa sync/sw_sync
Mode 0644.
Each open makes a software timeline of its own, which the descriptor's
.Dv SW_SYNC_IOC_CREATE_FENCE ,
.Dv SW_SYNC_IOC_INC
and
.Dv SW_SYNC_GET_DEADLINE
drive, as
.Xr dma-buf 7
gives them, and whose close completes its points not yet reached with
.Er ENOENT .
Linux's
.Dv CONFIG_SW_SYNC
gives the same file.
.It Pa sync/info
Mode 0444.
The live software timelines, oldest first, as Linux 6.12 writes them:
a header, then for each timeline a line of its name, the command of the
process that opened it, and its value, a line for each point not yet
reached, in the order of their values, and an empty line; then the
header of the sync files, with nothing under it, as on Linux:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
objs:
--------------
NAME: VALUE
  fence active: POINT / VALUE
\&
fences:
--------------
.Ed
.It Pa bkl/calls
Mode 0644.
The kernel's own counts of its entries and the big lock, a Rach2
file with no Linux counterpart: a header, then a line for each system call
by its number, each entry vector by its number, the takes after an idle
wait and after a switch, the latter by the system call slept in, each
kernel thread by its pid, with its name last, and each
.Xr ioctl 2
request by its number in hex, with any count:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kind number calls wall_us holds held_us wait_us
syscall 16 1200 5400 1200 3100 900
vector 32 800 0 800 120 40
take 1 0 0 700 9800 2100
slept 0 0 0 450 6300 1300
kthread 118 0 0 400 900 300 ki915
ioctl 0xc0406469 600 4100 600 2500 700
ioctl_lost 0
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar calls
counts a system call at its return and an interrupt or exception at its
entry, whether or not it takes the lock, each processor on counts of its
own, which a read sums;
.Ar wall_us
is a system call's time from before its entry chose the lock to its
return, its waits for the lock and its sleeps included, counted only with
.Ql rach2.cycleaccount
on the kernel's command line and 0 without it;
.Ar holds
and
.Ar held_us
are the holds of the lock that the entry began, from the take to the
give-back, as the lines
.Ql bkl_hold_entry
of
.Pa /proc/stat
count them: those of an entry that took the lock, and those a call or an
interrupt that runs without it took, charged to the entry the thread was
in;
.Ar wait_us
is the time those holds waited for the lock first.
The lines
.Ql take 0
and
.Ql take 1
count the holds a thread takes again after an idle wait and after its
switch, which have no calls.
The switch lets the lock go and the thread takes it back when it runs
again, whatever entry it is in, so
.Ql take 1
counts a system call's hold after each sleep.
A
.Ql slept
line divides
.Ql take 1
by the system call the thread slept in, by its number, with no calls and
no wall time: the rest of a call under the big lock after its sleeps.
Its holds count under
.Ql take 1
too.
A
.Ql kthread
line counts a kernel thread's own holds, those it takes outside any
interrupt or exception it is in: its first hold, its holds after each
sleep or idle wait, and those it takes in its code; they count there
alone, not under
.Ql take
or
.Ql vector 0
as well, so the lines of the file, the
.Ql slept
lines aside, never count one hold twice.
The processors' idle tasks count together, as one line named
.Ql idle .
The kernel threads named are the first 32 to take the lock since the last
zeroing; the holds of any after them count under
.Ql take
and
.Ql vector 0 .
The line
.Ql vector 0
counts, beside the divide error, the holds begun in no entry other than
after a switch: those of a thread in no entry that takes the lock again
after a run of a
.Xr kvm 4
guest, after another processor's claim or at a processor's start.
A system call numbered 512 or more counts as 512, a call of the i386 ABI
under its own number.
The times are microseconds, and 0 without an invariant time-stamp counter.
An
.Xr ioctl 2
request counts beside its system call, in 256 slots; the calls of a
request for which none of the 32 slots its hash names is free or its own
count in
.Ql ioctl_lost ,
which can count while most slots are free.
The block layer takes no big lock: each disk driver serializes on locks
of its own, and the AHCI, NVMe and ATA interrupts run without it.
Two drivers take it under the block layer.
USB storage takes it, only when it is free, to move a submitted request
from its intake into the stick's queue and start it; when it is held,
the USB pump, which runs under the lock, does that on its next pass.
A loop device takes it around each operation of the file system its
backing file is on, as a descriptor's call does, except a read of a file
system that reads without it.
A write of anything zeroes the counts.
.It Pa bkl/profile
Mode 0644.
A profile of the kernel under the big lock, a Rach2 file: each processor's
tick that finds the processor holding the lock, at 1000 Hz, takes a
sample of the instruction it stopped and the three return addresses above
it, from the frame chain, 0 past its end, with the entry the running
process is in; in the whole-kernel mode, which a write of
.Ql kernel
chooses, every tick that finds the processor in kernel mode outside an
idle halt takes one, with or without the lock: its innermost system call, interrupt or exception, as
.Pa bkl/calls
names it,
.Ql ioctl
and the request for an
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
or
.Ql kernel 0
for none, as in a kernel thread.
Code that runs with interrupts off is not sampled, nor is the writing of
this file's text; the rest of a read, its entry, allocation and copy out,
is sampled.
The text is the samples, which the lines' counts and the lost sum to; those lost, whose entry, instruction and callers
found none of the 32 slots their hash names among the 16384 free or their
own, which can happen while most slots are free; and a line for each
entry, instruction and callers sampled, with its count, in no order:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
samples 5120
lost 0
syscall 9 0xffffffff8012a3c4 0xffffffff80131f08 0xffffffff8013a210 0xffffffff80100e7c 37
ioctl 0xc0406469 0xffffffff8024b1d0 0xffffffff80250c44 0xffffffff8024e9a8 0xffffffff80245f10 12
.Ed
.Pp
The kernel's symbol table names the functions.
A write zeroes the profile and chooses its mode: the whole kernel for a
write that begins with
.Ql kernel ,
only while the thread whose ID follows it after a space runs, as in
.Ql kernel 791 ,
if one does, and the big lock's holds for any other.
A thread ID above 2147483647, or a write whose first 24 bytes end inside
the ID, fails with
.Er EINVAL
and leaves the profile as it was.
.It Pa bkl/long
Mode 0644.
The latest interrupt sample of each of the last 32 holds of the big lock
sampled at 16 ms or older, oldest first.
The header names the columns:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clock_us age_us cpu pid comm vector syscall pc[8]
.Ed
.Pp
The first two are the sample's TSC timestamp and the hold's age,
converted to microseconds at the calibrated invariant TSC rate.
The timestamp uses the same clock as
.Pa /proc/audio Ns 's
service clock-us, not uptime.
Without calibration there are no records.
The next columns name the processor, the interrupted thread (0 for none),
its command (a dash for none, spaces and nonprinting characters replaced
by question marks), and the hold's entry as
.Pa bkl/calls
charges the hold: its vector and system call number, -1 for none, or
.Ql take 0
for a take after an idle wait and
.Ql take 1
for a take after a switch, in the same two columns.
The eight hexadecimal PCs are the interrupted instruction and its
callers, zero after the end of the bounded kernel-stack walk.
Later interrupts during a hold replace its sample.
A hold that ends without an interrupt can leave no sample.
A read snapshots the ring once, before rendering it.
A write empties it; a hold already under way can be sampled again.
.It Pa bkl/tick
Mode 0644.
The parts of the timer tick, a Rach2 file: for each part, the ticks that
ran it, summed over the processors, their time in time-stamp counter cycles
and in microseconds, and the longest in microseconds.
The first line is
.Ql part calls cycles us max_us ,
and a line follows for each part, in the order the tick runs them:
.Ql clock ,
.Ql account ,
.Ql wake ,
.Ql hda ,
.Ql block_lag ,
.Ql block_request ,
.Ql speaker ,
.Ql lock ,
.Ql signals ,
.Ql load ,
.Ql netdev ,
.Ql netconsole ,
.Ql ath9k ,
.Ql tcp ,
.Ql ip_frag ,
.Ql ip6 ,
.Ql netlink ,
.Ql ip_loop ,
.Ql usb ,
.Ql input ,
.Ql platform ,
.Ql vkms ,
.Ql console ,
.Ql klog
and
.Ql nested .
.Pp
The first seven run without the big lock:
.Ql clock
is the count of the tick and the clock's pass, which the boot processor's
tick makes, or another processor's when the boot processor's is late,
and none while another processor is in it;
.Ql account
is the time charged to the process the tick interrupted, on every tick;
and
.Ql wake
is the timer pass, the sleepers and timers whose time has come, on the
tick that made the clock's pass; and
.Ql hda ,
.Ql block_lag ,
.Ql block_request
and
.Ql speaker
are the boot processor's periodic services that take only locks of their
own, on each of its ticks.
.Ql lock
is the tick's take of the big lock, its try and any wait, counted only on
a tick that took the lock; a try that failed is counted in no row.
The boot processor's tick tries at every tick and waits at its fourth
failed try in a row, and another processor's tries only while signals of
the tick wait to be posted.
The rows after it run under the lock:
.Ql signals ,
the posting of the signals of the tick, SIGALRM, SIGVTALRM, SIGPROF and
the POSIX timers', that the accounting and the timer pass left;
.Ql load ,
the load average and the idle count of the loopback ring; and the boot
processor's other periodic services, from
.Ql netdev
to
.Ql console ,
and
.Ql klog ,
the log records kept for a holder of the lock.
.Ql nested
counts the ticks whose locked part ran inside a hold that the code the
tick interrupted already had, with that part's time, which the other rows
count too and which is the interrupted hold's, not the tick's.
So the rows from
.Ql signals
to
.Ql klog ,
less
.Ql nested ,
sum to about the hold that
.Pa bkl/calls
charges to vector 32, from the take to the give-back; the wait that
.Ql lock
counts is not in it.
The microseconds are 0 without an invariant time-stamp counter; the cycles
are not.
A write of anything zeroes the counts: each processor zeroes its own at
its next tick, and a read counts them as zero until then.
.It Pa lockdep/inject
Mode 0600, only in the kernel built with
.Ql make rach2-lockdep .
Reads
.Ql abba irq ,
the words a write takes.
A write of
.Ql abba
takes two locks of its own in one order and then in the other, and
.Ql irq
takes one lock in an interrupt's context, a second with interrupts on,
and then the second while it holds the first, so that the lock-order checker reports a possible deadlock and an interrupt
inversion in the kernel log and counts them in
.Pa /proc/lockdep_stats .
Each report is made once a boot.
Another text fails with
.Er EINVAL .
Linux has no such file; its lib/locking-selftest.c makes such orders at
boot.
.It Pa test_lockup
Mode 0600.
Reads
.Ql soft hard hung ,
the kinds a write takes.
A write of
.Ql soft SECONDS
or
.Ql hard SECONDS
spins in the write with interrupts on or off, and
.Ql hung SECONDS
sleeps in it in the state D, for 1 to 600 seconds, so that the lockup
detectors of
.Xr lockup 7
report it; the write returns when the lockup ends.
Another text fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
and a second
.Ql hung
while one sleeps with
.Er EBUSY .
Linux has no such file; its lib/test_lockup.c is a module that takes the
same choices as parameters.
.It Pa exits
Mode 0444, on a PC booted with
.Ql rach2.exits ,
which alone has the file; a Rach2 file with no Linux counterpart.
The instructions each processor ran that a hypervisor may take as a VM
exit, counted since the first program started, one line for each:
.Ql KIND NUMBER TOTAL CPU0 CPU1 ... ,
where KIND is
.Ql rdmsr ,
.Ql wrmsr ,
.Ql cpuid ,
.Ql in ,
.Ql out
or
.Ql hlt ,
and NUMBER, in hexadecimal, the MSR, the leaf or the port, or 0 for
.Ql hlt .
Then a line
.Ql ipi KIND TOTAL CPU0 ...
for each kind of interprocessor interrupt each processor sent,
.Ql kick ,
.Ql tlb ,
.Ql kernel_tlb ,
.Ql barrier ,
.Ql space ,
.Ql call
and
.Ql stop ,
and
.Ql lost TOTAL CPU0 ... ,
the counts that found a processor's 64 kinds and numbers taken.
An access exits under KVM unless KVM gives the register to the guest, as
it gives the FS and GS bases, so the counts name the exits that KVM's
own counters
.Pq Xr kvm 4
leave unnamed.
.It Pa kvm/exit_stats
Mode 0644, present when
.Pa /dev/kvm
is.
The guests' exits since boot, counted by the processor's exit code, with
the TSC cycles of each kind's parts; Linux has no such file, and
counts its exits by code through its
.Dq kvm_exit
trace event, whose names the file uses.
A first line names the backend and the TSC's rate, a second the columns,
then each code with exits has a line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
backend vmx|svm tsc_khz KHZ
code exits kernel user wait_cycles handle_cycles prepare_cycles entry_cycles run_cycles exit_cycles fast name
0xCODE EXITS KERNEL USER WAIT HANDLE PREPARE ENTRY RUN EXIT FAST NAME
.Ed
.Pp
.Ar KERNEL
exits were handled in the kernel and the guest entered again;
.Ar USER
ended
.Dv KVM_RUN .
.Ar ENTRY
runs from just before the run loop enters the guest to the backend's entry
instruction: the guest's state loaded and the VMCS or VMCB brought up to
date;
.Ar RUN
from there to the exit, the processor's entry and exit and the guest's
own run;
.Ar EXIT
from the exit to the backend's return;
.Ar WAIT
is the part of the exit's handling spent waiting for the big kernel lock,
which an exit the kernel handles without it never does; and
.Ar HANDLE
the rest of the time from the backend's return to just before the next
entry, or to
.Dv KVM_RUN Ns 's
return.
.Ar PREPARE
is the part of
.Ar HANDLE
from the run loop's first pass over its top after the exit is handled:
a switch to another thread at the end of the slice, the requests, the
local APIC's work and the event to inject.
A wait for the big kernel lock inside it counts in
.Ar WAIT ,
as one in the rest of the handling does, and a preparation that ends on
a processor whose TSC reads lower than the one where it began counts 0.
A HLT the kernel handles only marks the vCPU halted; its sleep until an
interrupt comes at the loop's top, so both
.Ar HANDLE
and
.Ar PREPARE
count it.
.Ar ENTRY ,
.Ar RUN
and
.Ar EXIT
count the runs the backend timed, and
.Ar PREPARE
the exits handled in the kernel.
.Ar FAST
counts those of the
.Ar KERNEL
exits that the backend's fast path handled with interrupts still off,
entering the guest again without the run loop, as Linux's
.Fn vmx_exit_handlers_fastpath
and
.Fn svm_exit_handlers_fastpath
do: a WRMSR of the TSC deadline, and on VMX the preemption timer's
expiry.
Their
.Ar HANDLE
is the fast path's, and their
.Ar PREPARE
0.
An exit whose next entry does not come, because a request or an error
ends
.Dv KVM_RUN
first, ended
.Dv KVM_RUN .
Each processor keeps its own counts, and the file shows their sums.
The code is SVM's EXITCODE or VMX's exit reason, and a failed entry is
0xffffffff under SVM and 0x80000000 under VMX.
SVM's codes from 0xb0 to 0x3ff and above 0x40f, and VMX's from 0xb0 on, share
one line,
.Ql other ,
with code 0.
A write of
.Ql 0
clears every count, as a write of 0 clears one of Linux's KVM statistics
in its debugfs; any other write fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.It Pa dri
The display devices' files, one directory for each device registered with
the DRM core, as Linux 6.12 makes them.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE
The directory of one device, named as Linux names it: by its PCI function,
such as
.Pa 0000:00:02.0 ,
or by its platform device, such as
.Pa simple-framebuffer.0
or
.Pa vkms .
.It Pa dri/N , dri/M
Symbolic links to the device's directory, by the minor number of its card
node
.Pa /dev/dri/cardN
and, for a device with a render node, of
.Pa /dev/dri/renderDM ,
M = 128 + N, by which IGT finds the device of a descriptor.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/name
Mode 0444.
The driver's name, the device's,
the bus name of the master once its
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SET_VERSION
of interface 1.1 or later gave it,
and the device's name again:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bochs-drm dev=0000:00:02.0 master=pci:0000:00:02.0 unique=0000:00:02.0
.Ed
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/clients
Mode 0444.
A header, then a line for each open file of the device's nodes, the oldest
first: the command and thread group of the process that last used it, or
.Ql <unknown> ,
the node's minor number,
.Ql y
or
.Ql n
for whether it is the master and whether it is authenticated, the
process's effective uid, and its magic:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
             command  tgid dev master a   uid      magic
              weston   312   0   y    y     0          0
.Ed
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/gem_names
Mode 0444, for a device with GEM.
Each buffer object with a flink name, by name, with its size in bytes,
the handles to it and its references.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/framebuffer
Mode 0444, for a device with mode setting.
Each framebuffer, the newest first, as Linux's
.Fn drm_framebuffer_print_info
writes it: the command of its maker, its references, its format as a
fourcc, its modifier, its size, and each plane's size, pitch, offset and
buffer object.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/state
Mode 0444, for a device with atomic mode setting.
The state of each plane, then of each CRTC, then of each connector, as
Linux's
.Fn drm_state_dump
writes them.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/crtc-N
A directory for CRTC N of a device with mode setting, made for every CRTC
as Linux's
.Fn drm_debugfs_crtc_add
makes it; it holds the files below that its driver has.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/crtc-N/crc/control
Mode 0644, for a CRTC whose driver has CRCs, as
.Pa crc/data .
A write names the source of the CRTC's CRCs for the next open of
.Pa crc/data ;
.Er EINVAL
for a source the driver does not have,
.Er E2BIG
past 4095 bytes, and
.Er EBUSY
while
.Pa crc/data
is open.
A read gives each source the driver lists and takes, a line each, the
chosen one followed by
.Ql * ;
nothing for a driver that lists none.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/crtc-N/crc/data
Mode 0444, made only for a CRTC whose driver has CRCs, as Linux makes it.
The open starts the source, and fails with
.Er EIO
while the CRTC is off, and
.Er EBUSY
while another open has it; the last close stops it.
Each read waits for a frame, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
under
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
and gives one line of it, ended by a newline and a NUL: the frame number as
.Ql 0x%08x ,
or ten
.Ql X
for a source without one, and each value as
.Ql \ 0x%08x .
A buffer too short for the line fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLIN
and
.Dv POLLRDNORM
while a line waits.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/crtc-N/vblank_backwards
Mode 0444, for a CRTC with vblanks; Rach2's own, not Linux's.
How many times a vblank of the CRTC was stamped earlier than one stamped
before it, as a decimal number and a newline.
Every vblank, flip and sequence event and every
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_WAIT_VBLANK
reply carries the latest stamp, and a compositor such as Weston aborts on
one that goes back, so the number is 0 unless the driver's scanout
position read a later vblank as an earlier one.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/crtc-N/i915_pipe
Mode 0444, for a device bound by
.Xr i915 4 .
The CRTC's pipe as its letter and a newline, such as
.Ql A ,
by which IGT finds a CRTC's pipe, as Linux's.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/crtc-N/i915_current_bpc
Mode 0444, for the same.
.Ql Current: N
and a newline, N the bits a colour of the pipe's configuration, a third of
its bits a pixel.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/encoder-N/bridges
Mode 0444.
The bridges of the encoder's chain, which no driver has; empty.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/virtio_gpu_reset
Mode 0200, for a virtio GPU with virgl; see
.Xr virtio-gpu 4 .
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/CONNECTOR/force
Mode 0644, for each connector, by its name, such as
.Pa Virtual-1 .
Reads
.Ql unspecified ,
.Ql off ,
.Ql on
or
.Ql digital ;
a write of one of the four words, with a newline at most after it, sets
what the next probe of the connector reports, the first by detection;
anything else, or more than 11 bytes, fails with
.Er EINVAL .
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/CONNECTOR/edid_override
Mode 0644.
A write of an EDID, of the blocks its base block names, stands in for the
sink's from the next probe on, where the driver reads the sink's EDID or
finds no mode without one, as on Linux; vkms finds its modes without one
and keeps them.
A write of
.Ql reset
drops it;
.Er EINVAL
for bytes that are not a valid EDID.
A read gives the override's bytes, or nothing.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_capabilities
Mode 0444, for a device bound by
.Xr i915 4 ,
as are the files below that begin with
.Pa i915_ .
The platform's name, its graphics, media and display versions, the
device's flags, the GT's information and the driver's capabilities, one
.Ql name: value
a line, in Linux's order.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_engine_info
Mode 0444.
The GT's awake count and the CS timestamp's frequency, then for each
running engine its state, registers, requests on the chip with the
commands of each, status page and interrupts, as Linux's
.Fn intel_engine_dump
prints them.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_gem_objects
Mode 0444.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
N shrinkable [0 free] objects, BYTES bytes
system: total:0x%016x bytes
.Ed
.Pp
and a line for stolen memory when the device has it.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_frequency_info
Mode 0444.
The GT's frequency registers and limits, as Linux's
.Fn rps_frequency_dump .
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_rps_boost_info
Mode 0444.
Whether RPS runs and the GT is busy, the wait boosts outstanding, the page
flips pending that hold RPS at high power, the requested and actual
frequency with its limits, in MHz, and the wait boosts counted, as Linux's
.Fn i915_rps_boost_info .
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_cdclk_info
Mode 0444.
The current and the highest CD clock and the highest dot clock, in kHz.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_wedged
Mode 0644.
Reads 1 while the GPU is wedged, else 0.
A write of a number, in any base
.Fn kstrtoull
takes, resets the engines its bits name by Linux's engine ids, as a hang
does: the error state is captured, then each engine reset alone, or the
whole GPU when that fails; a reset that succeeds ends a wedge.
.Er EINVAL
for anything that is not such a number.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_gem_drop_caches
Mode 0644.
Reads
.Ql 0x000003ff ,
the bits a write takes, which are Linux's:
.Dv DROP_UNBOUND
(0x1) and
.Dv DROP_BOUND
(0x2) purge the objects marked
.Dv I915_MADV_DONTNEED
without and with bindings,
.Dv DROP_RETIRE
(0x4) retires the requests that have completed,
.Dv DROP_ACTIVE
(0x8) and
.Dv DROP_IDLE
(0x40) wait until the GPU is idle,
.Dv DROP_SHRINK_ALL
(0x20) purges both kinds, and
.Dv DROP_RESET_ACTIVE
(0x80) wedges a GPU that is not idle within 200 ms and resets it after.
.Dv DROP_FREED ,
.Dv DROP_RESET_SEQNO
and
.Dv DROP_RCU
do nothing.
The wait for idle ends with
.Er EINTR
at a signal.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_params
A directory with a file for each of the driver's and the display's
parameters, as Linux has, each giving its value and a newline, a boolean as
.Ql Y
or
.Ql N .
Two are written, mode 0600, by Linux's rules:
.Pa reset ,
a number in any base
.Fn kstrtouint
takes or a boolean: 0 makes a hang wedge the GPU, 1 resets the whole GPU
alone, and 2 or more, the default 3, an engine first;
.Pa enable_hangcheck ,
a boolean as
.Fn kstrtobool
reads it, which off stops the heartbeat that finds hangs and makes new
contexts not persistent.
Turned on again, an engine has its heartbeat back once it next goes from
idle to busy, or the GPU next wakes; an engine kept busy all the while has
none until then, as on Linux.
The rest are read-only, mode 0400, at Linux's defaults.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_error_state
Mode 0644.
The text of the first error state captured at a hang since boot or the
last clear, in Linux's format, with the status page and ring of each engine
with an incomplete request, hung or not, in Linux's ascii85; empty when
there is none.
The text is made at the open.
Any write clears the state.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_gpu_info
Mode 0644.
The same text for a state captured of every engine at the open, without
a hang.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_forcewake_user
Mode 0400.
An open holds the GT's forcewake, and so keeps the GT out of RC6, until
the last close, as Linux's does, so that a program may read and write the
registers through the mapping of BAR 0's
.Pa resource0
file of
.Xr sysfs 5 ,
as IGT's register access does.
It has no text.
.It Pa dri/DEVICE/i915_fake
Mode 0600.
Present for a device bound to the fake chip of
.Xr i915 4 ,
under
.Cm rach2.i915fake .
A read gives a line for each fault and each ring, the resets of each
ring's domain alone and of the whole GPU through
.Dv GEN6_GDRST ,
the device's messages, and the result of the last first light:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fault NAME armed COUNT injected COUNT
ring rcs0 commands COUNT batches COUNT hung 0|1
resets rcs0 COUNT bcs0 COUNT vcs0 COUNT vecs0 COUNT full COUNT
messages COUNT
light never|ok|error -ERRNO
.Ed
.Pp
A write of one command, 63 bytes at most, arms a fault:
.Ql NAME
once,
.Ql NAME N
.Ar N
times, 0 to disarm it, and
.Ql NAME always
for every time.
.Ql submit
fails the next submission to a ring with
.Er EIO ,
as a wedged GPU does;
.Ql bind
fails the next bind into the global GTT with
.Er ENOSPC ;
.Ql hang
hangs the ring at the next command it would run, until a reset of the
ring's domain;
.Ql irq
loses the next user interrupt, whose sequence number is still written.
.Ql clear
disarms every fault, and
.Ql light
runs first light on the render ring now, as
.Cm rach2.i915.firstlight
does at boot.
Any other command fails with
.Er EINVAL .
The text is made at the open and again after each write, and a read gives
it from the description's offset.
The kernel logs each injection as
.Dq i915: fake: injected: NAME .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
As root:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# ls /sys/kernel/debug/sync
info     sw_sync
# cat /sys/kernel/debug/sync/info
